WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.600
Shopify is one of the most incredible&nbsp;
technology platforms to be built in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:08.320
21st century. Nearly 2 million businesses&nbsp;
use Shopify to sell their products online,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:13.520
and that number is only growing. The company made&nbsp;
nearly $3 billion dollars last year and is now the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:19.120
most valuable corporation in all of Canada. It’s&nbsp;
an incredible growth story and at the heart of it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:24.720
all is the Shopify founder and CEO, Tobi Lutke.
Tobi isn’t like most big tech CEOs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:29.280
Even though he’s worth billions of dollars, he’s&nbsp;
somehow very relatable. When Mark Zuckerberg&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:33.440
live streamed himself barbecuing in the&nbsp;
backyard, pretty much everyone cringed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:37.360
and instantly turned it into a meme.
But when Tobi live streamed himself playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:42.560
StarCraft, people loved it. He actually got&nbsp;
invited to talk about StarCraft on The Pylon Show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:48.480
and spent 2 and a half hours just casually talking&nbsp;
about the game he loves so much. It was remarkably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:53.680
refreshing in a world where so many CEOs only do&nbsp;
press events when they’re launching new products,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:59.120
and avoid anything that could be off topic.
Whenever someone builds a massively successful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:03.680
business like Shopify, everyone wants to know&nbsp;
the secret of how they did it. And Tobi has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:07.200
answered this question directly:
“Because I’m a computer programmer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:11.680
I actually succeeded in this. But looking&nbsp;
back, I was saying, I only succeeded in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:15.200
this because I was a computer programmer”
But I don’t think that answer tells the full&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:19.440
story. There are plenty of computer programmers&nbsp;
who try to build great companies, only to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:24.480
crash and burn. Tobi clearly has a different&nbsp;
mental model for building companies than most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:29.840
entrepreneurs, and I wanted to know exactly how&nbsp;
he thinks, so I studied the history of Shopify and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:36.480
boiled his strategy down to a few key points.
In 1980, Tobi was born in a small town in Germany.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:40.480
Like lots of other successful technology&nbsp;
entrepreneurs, he started playing with computers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:46.000
at a very young age. When he was just six years&nbsp;
old, his parents gave him this computer. It looks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:51.360
ridiculously old by modern standards, but at the&nbsp;
time, it was incredibly versatile. Not only could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:56.160
Tobi play games on this thing, but he could use&nbsp;
it to program, and that’s exactly what he did.
&nbsp;

00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:00.960
By the time he was 11 years old, Tobi was&nbsp;
hacking games and building his own levels.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:06.000
We basically saw this exact same story play out&nbsp;
with Tim Sweeney in my video about Epic Games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:11.040
it’s a really common path for entrepreneurs, and&nbsp;
something I did myself when I was growing up.
&nbsp;

00:02:11.040 --> 00:02:15.200
Lots of tech entrepreneurs fall in love with&nbsp;
computers while playing video games as children,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:19.920
but most of them grow out of it and start&nbsp;
focusing entirely on business as they get older.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:24.880
Not Tobi though, he actually claims that video&nbsp;
games taught him more about managing a business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:30.240
than anything else. And his gaming experience will&nbsp;
become really important later in this story.
&nbsp;

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:34.960
By the time Tobi turned 17 years old, he was&nbsp;
completely obsessed with programming. Instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:39.680
of preparing for college, he chose to drop out&nbsp;
of high school and become an apprentice at one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:44.080
of the leading German tech companies, Siemens.&nbsp;
This experience allowed him to develop some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:48.640
really solid programming skills and start&nbsp;
making decent money, but he wasn’t very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:52.880
happy with the projects he was working on.
He was forced to build accounting software for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:57.665
old-school companies using Java and this just&nbsp;
wasn’t scratching his itch to work on cutting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.665 --> 00:03:02.560
edge technologies. But then he had an idea. 
He’d been doing a lot of snowboarding in Canada&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:07.120
and realized that there weren’t really any good&nbsp;
online stores selling high quality snowboarding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:12.320
gear at the time. This was 2004 after all and&nbsp;
Amazon was still pretty small. You could get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.320 --> 00:03:15.920
basic gear at places like Sports&nbsp;
Authority, but finding the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:20.400
gear required a lot of hunting around. 
So Tobi and a snowboarding buddy of his named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:24.400
Scott Lake decided to set up an online store&nbsp;
where they could sell their favorite gear.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:29.040
They called it Snowdevil.ca, and the site&nbsp;
is actually still online today. They have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:34.240
a funny quote in their about us section that&nbsp;
says: “Snowdevil is not your typical snowboard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:38.480
store.” Which sounds an awful lot like many&nbsp;
of the popular stores on Shopify today.
&nbsp;

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:42.960
Building this site was not particularly easy&nbsp;
though. Tobi had to cobble together a bunch of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:47.440
different legacy web technologies in order to get&nbsp;
it all to work. He did eventually get the store&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:52.080
online, but it was clear that a turnkey solution&nbsp;
would have been a much better experience.
&nbsp;

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:56.640
But then something really important happened. One&nbsp;
of Tobi’s friends told him about a new open-source&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:00.880
web project called Ruby on Rails. There was&nbsp;
only a small team of developers working on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:05.840
it back in 2004, but Tobi saw the potential and&nbsp;
started collaborating with the core team. 
&nbsp;

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:10.720
In order to test out what Ruby on Rails could&nbsp;
do, and contribute to the ecosystem, Tobi built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:16.320
an open-source blogging platform called Typo.&nbsp;
This wound up getting over 10,000 installs and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:21.040
becoming really popular in the Rails community.&nbsp;
It’s funny because, even though Tobi now runs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:27.200
a company worth over $100 billion dollars, he&nbsp;
still has “creator of the Typo weblog engine”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:30.720
listed on his LinkedIn profile. 
With all of this positive experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:35.520
using Ruby on Rails, Tobi decided to rebuild&nbsp;
the Snow Devil ecommerce store from scratch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:40.560
And this was a really crucial moment. Shopify&nbsp;
still uses Ruby on Rails today and by picking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:45.440
Ruby as a programming language and Rails as a web&nbsp;
framework, they were able to really accelerate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.440 --> 00:04:49.600
their development for years to come. And this&nbsp;
highlights one of Tobi’s key strengths as an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:53.760
entrepreneur: he’s a deeply technical founder. 
Lots of entrepreneurs learn enough about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:58.960
programming to build a basic minimum viable&nbsp;
product and start managing software engineers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:03.280
but Tobi became a true expert in Ruby&nbsp;
on Rails before launching Shopify. 
&nbsp;

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:07.680
See, Tobi knew that, even though Ruby on&nbsp;
Rails could speed up the development time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:12.880
for new ecommerce sites, it wasn’t fast enough.&nbsp;
Most people who wanted to sell things online&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:17.360
didn’t want to write any code at all, so&nbsp;
Tobi pivoted his snowboarding business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.360 --> 00:05:21.360
into what would become Shopify.
Tobi and his co-founder Scott took all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:25.920
the money they had made selling snowboards, along&nbsp;
with some small investments from family members,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:29.760
and started turning their snowboarding&nbsp;
website into a proper ecommerce platform.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:34.000
They spent nearly 18 months developing the first&nbsp;
version of Shopify, and when they launched it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:38.720
Tobi used an online name generator to come up with&nbsp;
the brand name. The company was originally called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:43.840
“Jaded Pixel” which, I think we can all agree is a&nbsp;
lot less catchy than Shopify. But that name didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:48.240
last very long. Scott had more experience&nbsp;
than Tobi running businesses and knew that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:53.360
they needed something catchy, so he decided to&nbsp;
combine the words “simplify” and “shopping” to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:58.480
create “Shopify”. Amazingly, the domain name was&nbsp;
available, so they switched over immediately.
&nbsp;

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:03.440
Their 2006 launch went fairly smoothly,&nbsp;
although Tobi and his team didn’t really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:07.760
know exactly how big Shopify could get. They’d&nbsp;
delivered a great product that allowed merchants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:12.800
to set up a website quickly, customize a theme,&nbsp;
track their orders, and manage their inventory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:16.880
all without writing a single line of code.&nbsp;
The only problem was the business model.
&nbsp;

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:22.160
Instead of charging Shopify merchants a flat fee&nbsp;
like they do now, Tobi wanted to take a percentage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:27.200
of revenue from each store. This model works great&nbsp;
for small businesses, but as these companies start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:32.320
to grow, the Shopify fees can really add&nbsp;
up. Customers had to pay 3% of their sales&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:36.880
to Shopify and the cost of using the platform&nbsp;
could skyrocket when businesses got big. 
&nbsp;

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:41.120
Now, there weren’t really all that many big&nbsp;
companies using the platform at that time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:44.800
but every entrepreneur is optimistic&nbsp;
about their business, and the idea of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:48.960
paying Shopify millions of dollars in&nbsp;
fees once their business got huge made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:54.720
them hesitant to sign up in the first place.
So Tobi changed the model. Shopify switched to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.720 --> 00:07:00.400
flat-rate pricing in 2007 and merchants started&nbsp;
using the platform in droves. The growth allowed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:05.360
them to raise $250 thousand dollars from an&nbsp;
angel investor named John Phillips. This was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:09.840
really critical moment for the company because,&nbsp;
up until this point, Tobi had actually been the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:14.880
chief technology officer and Scott Lake had&nbsp;
been CEO. But after this funding came through,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:19.680
Scott left the company to work on a new&nbsp;
venture and Tobi had to take over as CEO.
&nbsp;

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:24.080
This was a big shift for Tobi, who had never&nbsp;
really been in a management position before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:28.080
and was primarily focused on just building&nbsp;
great software. But John Phillips stepped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:32.480
in as a mentor to Tobi and the results have&nbsp;
been really spectacular. Tobi has leveraged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.480 --> 00:07:37.360
his deep understanding of software engineering&nbsp;
into a successful leadership model. Developers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:41.520
want to work with people who understand the&nbsp;
craft of software development and Tobi is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:46.160
perfect engineering manager in this regard. Even&nbsp;
though Tobi was still in his twenties, he became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:50.800
a great leader because of his ability to look&nbsp;
at problems from an engineering perspective.
&nbsp;

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:56.240
In his first year as CEO, Tobi did a great job:&nbsp;
he grew the team to 10 people, got the company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.240 --> 00:08:01.600
to a million dollars in revenue, and was able&nbsp;
to become cash flow positive. They also landed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.600 --> 00:08:06.640
Tesla as a customer, which is pretty crazy to&nbsp;
think about considering how small both companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:12.160
were back then and where they are now.
Even with all this success, Tobi was still unsure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:16.880
about where Shopify would go. He enjoyed running&nbsp;
the Snowboarding store as a lifestyle business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:21.200
and if they were growing profitably, maybe they&nbsp;
didn’t need to go the traditional venture capital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:26.000
route, but then something incredible happened.
Raising venture capital only makes sense when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:31.280
have a huge addressable market and are absolutely&nbsp;
sure that more money can actually accelerate your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:37.200
growth. So, in a typical engineering fashion,&nbsp;
Tobi ran an experiment to evaluate the impact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:41.680
additional capital would have on Shopify’s&nbsp;
growth trajectory. He split a small marketing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:47.520
budget across a few different tests and they&nbsp;
all outperformed expectations. One test was to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:53.040
incentivize web design agencies with a referral&nbsp;
program. Shopify would give web designers a share&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:58.640
of future profits if they used Shopify as their&nbsp;
ecommerce platform of choice. Tobi also partnered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:03.920
with Tim Ferriss to run a business competition to&nbsp;
promote the platform. Essentially, whoever built&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:09.760
the biggest business on Shopify would get a $100&nbsp;
thousand dollar bonus from Shopify directly. Tobi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:14.640
really couldn’t spare the extra cash, since&nbsp;
they hadn’t raised proper venture capital yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:19.040
but he took the risk anyway and it paid&nbsp;
off massively. The companies that joined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:23.360
the competition wound up generating over&nbsp;
$3 million dollars in revenue and brought a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:27.760
bunch of positive attention to the company.
These successful growth tests really changed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:31.440
Tobi’s perspective on the business. It was&nbsp;
clear that there was room in the market for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:36.320
an ecommerce platform like Shopify. As big as&nbsp;
Amazon was in 2010, companies still wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:40.880
be able to own their customer relationship,&nbsp;
and Shopify made this dead simple to do.
&nbsp;

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:46.080
So, Tobi raised a $7 million dollar Series&nbsp;
A, led by Bessemer Venture Partners. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:50.400
cash injection allowed Tobi to really expand&nbsp;
the vision for what Shopify could become.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:55.200
They developed an API that let 3rd party&nbsp;
developers build apps and integrations to solve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.200 --> 00:10:00.320
any problem a customer might have. This app store&nbsp;
became incredibly important in making Shopify a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:05.200
real platform and Tobi actually mentioned&nbsp;
that he got this idea from Bill Gates. 
&nbsp;

00:10:05.200 --> 00:10:11.040
When he was just 16, Tobi read a book about Bill&nbsp;
Gates and learned a pretty significant insight.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:16.400
See, back in 2010, every company wanted to be a&nbsp;
“platform” company. All the hot social networks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.400 --> 00:10:21.040
like Facebook and Twitter were platforms with&nbsp;
network effects and had grown to enormous scale.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:26.000
With a company like Shopify, there isn’t really&nbsp;
a network effect, but that’s where Bill Gates’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:30.960
insight about platforms comes in. Bill said&nbsp;
that “You’re only really a platform if the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:36.720
value of the ecosystem on top of the platform is&nbsp;
larger than the company that owns the platform.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:41.360
That’s certainly true for Microsoft Windows,&nbsp;
and it would quickly become true for Shopify.
&nbsp;

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:44.240
And this is Tobi’s second key&nbsp;
strength as an entrepreneur:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:49.120
he’s constantly evolving his product offering.
When Shopify first launched, the value&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:54.880
propositions listed on their landing page were:&nbsp;
hosting, bandwidth, and security. Today, that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.880 --> 00:11:01.280
table stakes, so Shopify now focuses on featuring&nbsp;
beautiful templates, smooth payment processing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.280 --> 00:11:05.600
and a world-class plugin ecosystem.
The idea that Shopify could be an ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:10.160
evolving platform and eventually&nbsp;
cater to every type of online store&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.160 --> 00:11:15.040
really changed the company’s trajectory. When&nbsp;
Tobi raised his Series A at the end of 2010,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:21.040
the company had about 20 employees. One year&nbsp;
later, Shopify had over 100 employees. 
&nbsp;

00:11:21.040 --> 00:11:27.440
Sales growth took off as well. In just 12 months,&nbsp;
they went from generating $275 million dollars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:34.160
across 10,000 merchants to more than $750&nbsp;
million dollars across 40,000 merchants. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.160 --> 00:11:39.280
Shopify only keeps a small percentage of that,&nbsp;
but it was still enough for them to pull in $24&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:44.320
million in net revenue. But the growth didn’t&nbsp;
stop there. Next year they were able to double&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.320 --> 00:11:48.640
their net revenue to $50 million dollars. This is&nbsp;
exactly what you want to see if you’re running a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.640 --> 00:11:52.720
venture backed Software-as-a-Service company.
You can’t stop at $50 million dollars though.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.720 --> 00:11:56.880
When you’re venture backed, you’re aiming&nbsp;
for an IPO, and Tobi knew that the market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.880 --> 00:12:02.800
for Shopify was huge, so he kept expanding.&nbsp;
In 2013, Shopify launched Point of Sale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.800 --> 00:12:06.560
which would allow merchants to run their&nbsp;
physical retail stores using the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.560 --> 00:12:10.640
software that powered their website. More and&nbsp;
more direct to consumer e-commerce companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.640 --> 00:12:15.520
were opening physical storefronts, so having&nbsp;
a product that could handle both online and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.520 --> 00:12:20.400
offline sales was a huge benefit.
Then, a year later, Tobi and his team&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.400 --> 00:12:25.680
launched Shopify Plus. I was actually one&nbsp;
of the first Shopify merchants to join Plus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:30.240
and it was a game changer for me. At the time,&nbsp;
my company Soylent was growing really fast. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.240 --> 00:12:34.320
were scheduled to appear on the Colbert Report&nbsp;
to discuss the product and we knew we needed our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:39.280
ecommerce site to perform under immense traffic.&nbsp;
Appearing on national TV in front of an audience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.280 --> 00:12:44.240
of millions drove an insane amount of customers&nbsp;
to our site and keeping our own servers online&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.240 --> 00:12:49.440
would have been incredibly difficult, but because&nbsp;
we were on Shopify, everything went smoothly and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.440 --> 00:12:54.320
was able to enjoy the show and the after party&nbsp;
without worrying about the site going down.
&nbsp;

00:12:54.320 --> 00:12:58.320
Shopify Plus allowed bigger merchants&nbsp;
to use the platform with confidence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.320 --> 00:13:03.120
Plus had uptime guarantees and a bunch of&nbsp;
professional level features that were essential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.120 --> 00:13:08.560
for larger organizations. They could still service&nbsp;
small mom and pop shops, but now big companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:14.720
like Unilever and Google could use Shopify as&nbsp;
well. This propelled Shopify to an IPO in 2015,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:20.080
which valued them at $1.3 billion dollars.&nbsp;
That was great for a relatively young company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.080 --> 00:13:25.760
but the stock has grown by more than 100x&nbsp;
since the IPO. Incredible performance.
&nbsp;

00:13:25.760 --> 00:13:30.320
There are a few obvious reasons for this&nbsp;
growth: Shopify was using the best technology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.320 --> 00:13:35.120
they rode the massive wave of ecommerce growth,&nbsp;
and they didn’t have all that many significant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.120 --> 00:13:40.240
competitors. But I think the true reason for&nbsp;
Shopify’s success comes back to Tobi Lutke. 
&nbsp;

00:13:40.240 --> 00:13:45.120
I’ve met a lot of successful entrepreneurs, but&nbsp;
Tobi stands out to me as the most adaptable of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:48.960
them all. And there are a few great&nbsp;
stories to illustrate this trait:
&nbsp;

00:13:48.960 --> 00:13:53.520
As he was scaling Shopify, Tobi needed&nbsp;
to upgrade his office space in Ottawa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:57.840
to accommodate the next wave of hires. The&nbsp;
lease was running out on his current building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.840 --> 00:14:03.760
and the next place wasn’t move-in ready yet, so&nbsp;
they were in a jam. As a plan B, Tobi prepared the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.760 --> 00:14:08.640
team to work from home in case they got stuck&nbsp;
without a place to work for a while. The new,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.640 --> 00:14:13.120
bigger office wound up being ready on time,&nbsp;
so they could have just moved in without any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:13.120 --> 00:14:18.800
disruption, but Tobi had another idea. He wanted&nbsp;
to see if working from home could be viable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.800 --> 00:14:23.280
so he told everyone to spend a month working&nbsp;
remotely, even though they had a fully functioning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.280 --> 00:14:28.400
office available. This was all before COVID, but&nbsp;
the experience made the company more flexible and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.400 --> 00:14:33.360
they learned a lot about how they collaborate.
This flexibility also played a big role during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.360 --> 00:14:38.400
Shopify Developer Conference in 2017. They were&nbsp;
hosting the event in San Francisco and there was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.400 --> 00:14:43.920
an unexpected power outage. The entire building&nbsp;
was closed off to visitors, but instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:48.560
cancelling all the sessions for the day, they just&nbsp;
held the conference outside in the parking lot.
&nbsp;

00:14:48.560 --> 00:14:53.600
Most people would be phased by unexpected events&nbsp;
like this, but Tobi is at his best in these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.600 --> 00:14:59.760
situations. And that’s Tobi’s third key advantage&nbsp;
as an entrepreneur: he thrives on change. 
&nbsp;

00:14:59.760 --> 00:15:04.320
This all comes back to his experience as a&nbsp;
StarCraft player. Winning a competitive RTS&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.320 --> 00:15:10.080
game like StarCraft requires managing lots of&nbsp;
different resources with imperfect information.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:13.600
And I’m not just talking about&nbsp;
in-game resources like minerals.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.600 --> 00:15:18.880
The most important resource is actually your own&nbsp;
attention. The game is incredibly fast-paced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.880 --> 00:15:23.120
and where you spend your limited attention&nbsp;
will determine whether or not you win. 
&nbsp;

00:15:23.120 --> 00:15:28.000
Tobi is a master of allocating his attention&nbsp;
to the highest value opportunity at Shopify,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:32.400
and that’s why the company is so successful&nbsp;
today. So I encourage you to think like Tobi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.400 --> 00:15:36.480
as you're building your startup, and maybe go play&nbsp;
some StarCraft when you need to clear your head.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.480 --> 00:15:39.600
I’m more of a Counter-Strike guy&nbsp;
myself, but to each their own.
&nbsp;

00:15:39.600 --> 00:15:42.800
If you want to chat with me, you&nbsp;
can always just DM me on Twitter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.800 --> 00:15:49.760
and please check out this recommended video, the&nbsp;
YouTube algorithm thinks you’ll really like it. 

